<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The one destructive control on the settings screen ("Clear token"). Same
  geometry as @drawable/outline_button_bg — 22dp radius, 1dp ring, no fill at
  rest — so it is recognisably a button of the same family, with the ring and
  the label in @color/dicta_error instead of @color/dicta_outline.

  🔴 The colour is the WEAKEST of the three cues here and is deliberately not
  the only one, per the rule in colors.xml ("state is never carried by colour
  alone"), which applies to consequence as much as to state: a red ring is
  invisible to a red-blind operator and invisible again in a greyscale
  screenshot. The other two cues are in the layout and carry the meaning on
  their own — the ✕ glyph (@drawable/ic_close) as drawableStart, and a
  contentDescription (@string/stt_clear_token_desc) that names what is deleted
  and what happens next. Removing either of those, and leaving only this
  drawable, would make the control's destructiveness colour-only.

  dicta_error measures 6.68:1 on surface_2 per colors.xml and better on the
  level-0 window this button is actually drawn over, so it clears 1.4.11's 3:1
  for a component boundary as well as AA for the label text.

  Pressed fills to surface_3, exactly as outline_button_bg does, rather than to
  an error-tinted fill: a saturated red flash at full button width reads as a
  failure that has already happened rather than as a press.
-->
<selector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <item android:state_pressed="true">
        <shape android:shape="rectangle">
            <solid android:color="@color/dicta_surface_3" />
            <corners android:radius="22dp" />
            <stroke android:width="1dp" android:color="@color/dicta_error" />
        </shape>
    </item>
    <item>
        <shape android:shape="rectangle">
            <solid android:color="@android:color/transparent" />
            <corners android:radius="22dp" />
            <stroke android:width="1dp" android:color="@color/dicta_error" />
        </shape>
    </item>
</selector>
